"""Forty-four original Osean continuations; institutions are authored gameplay."""
from pacific_nations.model import Branch

TAG='OSE';NAME='Osea'
OPENING_TITLE='The Promise Outside the Room'
OPENING="""The hearing has ended, but a transport clerk is still waiting outside with a cancelled receiving slot. Inside, the committee has agreed that every commitment must have an accountable office. Outside, somebody has to telephone a workshop and explain why its people will be waiting another week. The clerk is invited back. She places her schedule beside the polished final return and asks which one the government intends to keep. A defence liaison draws up a chair rather than answering for her. The new review will begin with the promise that did not fit inside the old account."""
CONCLUSION_TITLE='What the Next Shift Receives'
CONCLUSION="""The final folder is thinner than the committee expected. Its clerks have removed duplicate instructions, unsigned assurances and reports that described intended work as work delivered. The disagreements remain. A public representative still objects to a protected schedule; the receiving officer still believes the objection understates the risk of delay. Both have signed beside the decision they actually made. There is room at the back for the next shift's correction. Whether the wider world has accepted a dialogue, refused it or become more dangerous, the government can leave this account to its successors without requiring them to pretend every promise was fulfilled."""
FINAL_REQUIREMENT='has_country_flag = OSE_snc_guarantor_limits_review_done has_country_flag = OSE_snc_contact_verification_review_done'
PUBLIC_TRIGGERS={
 'ac_snc_ose_verification_earned':'has_country_flag = OSE_snc_contact_verification_settlement_done OR = { AND = { has_country_flag = OSE_snc_contact_verification_civil_done has_country_flag = ac_snc_ose_message_11_public } AND = { has_country_flag = OSE_snc_contact_verification_protected_done has_country_flag = OSE_snc_contact_verification_staff_return_done has_country_flag = ac_snc_ose_message_21_staff } }',
 'ac_snc_ose_channel_earned':'has_country_flag = OSE_snc_pacific_channel_settlement_done',
 'ac_snc_ose_science_channel_earned':'has_country_flag = OSE_snc_pacific_channel_settlement_done has_country_flag = OSE_snp_civil_space_service_completed country_exists = YUK YUK = { original_tag = YUK has_country_flag = ac_yuk_arkbird_partner } has_global_flag = ac_sp_arkbird_complete',
}

BRANCHES=(
Branch(TAG,'guarantor_limits','guarantor_review','2006.1.1',('assembly','scales'),(
 ('Read the Unkept Promise',"""A guarantor's letter has reached three departments and acquired three different meanings. The foreign desk describes reassurance, the military office describes a contingency, and the transport clerk has reserved an actual receiving window. None has knowingly lied. The original limitation disappeared as each office explained the letter to the next. Bring the versions together before issuing another assurance. The government must decide which promises it can sustain, who may change their meaning, and how the people affected will learn that a commitment has reached its limit."""),
 ('Publish the Receiving Limit',"""The civil representative wants the limit printed beside the promise, where a mayor or workshop steward can find it without knowing somebody in the ministry. Publication will invite arguments that a quiet qualification might have avoided. It will also make it harder for an urgent military request to become a permanent exception by moving between offices. Accept that political burden and give the receiving services a public reference. Their protected time will be defended in the open, including when the government would prefer to discuss only the reassurance it has offered abroad."""),
 ('Keep a Protected Undertaking',"""The defence liaison asks for a different kind of honesty: an undertaking small enough to meet, with staff who cannot be reassigned when another department discovers a pressing need. Civil services will carry the resulting delay. The liaison agrees that their names belong in the same account, rather than a separate explanation nobody abroad will read. Protect the professional schedule while recording what it displaces. A dependable commitment can strengthen national readiness, but it must not draw its credibility from the convenient disappearance of the people waiting outside its boundary."""),
 ('Name Who May Amend It',"""An amendment arrives with the original authorization number and an additional paragraph. The receiving office cannot tell whether it replaces the earlier promise or adds another one. Require a named amending authority and open a fresh review of the selected responsibility. The period already described in an old report cannot answer for this new instruction. The committee will wait for the corresponding funded work to be completed after its request, then compare the actual receiving return with the undertaking that somebody was willing to sign at the beginning."""),
 ('Compare Promise and Return',"""The receiving report includes the cancelled window that started the dispute. It also records a delivery that reached its intended office without emergency intervention. The committee declines to average the two into a statement that everything is satisfactory. Ask which part of the undertaking worked, which cost was accepted, and whether an official changed the condition while somebody else was already relying on it. Only a genuinely completed period belongs in this review. Its uncomfortable details are the reason to conduct an assessment rather than simply renew the original wording."""),
 ('Let the Objection Stand',"""A civil representative refuses to withdraw her objection merely because the receiving work is complete. She agrees that the government met the revised undertaking; she does not agree that it chose the right undertaking. Leave both statements together. The public account will take more political attention to defend, but future officers will be able to distinguish an accepted cost from unanimous approval. This is not a veto over every protected commitment. It is a refusal to make an accurate delivery report perform the additional task of erasing a legitimate disagreement."""),
 ('Preserve the Staff Dissent',"""The professional return contains a dissenting signature from an officer who believed the protected schedule left too little margin. His colleagues ask to keep the discussion within the receiving chain, where its technical detail will not be mistaken for a withdrawn guarantee. Retain a signed staff account and the obligation to answer it. The route protects professional continuity while reserving civilian capacity for that work. It cannot claim public agreement, and it must not punish the person whose objection made the final undertaking more precise than the first."""),
 ('A Guarantee with an Edge',"""The revised guarantee has an edge that can be found without opening a second folder. It identifies the authority, the receiving responsibility, and the procedure for saying that the undertaking has reached its limit. The committee keeps its selected public or protected account rather than pretending both were adopted. The completed review improves the standing practice, while the accepted political or civilian burden remains. A foreign refusal can still leave this a useful national settlement: the government has learned how to make a smaller promise credible before it asks anybody else to trust a larger one."""),
),(
 ('Published receiving limit',{'stability_factor':.02},{'political_power_gain':-.04}),
 ('Protected undertaking',{'army_org_factor':.02},{'consumer_goods_factor':.005}),
),
"""The clerk has brought the telephone log. Three calls concern the same assurance, and each caller believes a different condition applies. The defence liaison starts to explain that the wording was clear at the time. The clerk turns the log around. 'Clear to which receiving office?' Nobody laughs. A civil representative offers to read the limit aloud before the next promise is sent; the liaison asks that technical reservations remain attached to the version his staff must deliver. The chair leaves both suggestions open. Somebody will have to choose which account the government is prepared to defend.""",
"""The receiving officer reads the completed period without replacing its missing entries. A delivery arrived, another task waited, and a qualification reached its intended office before anyone acted on it. 'That last one is progress,' the clerk says. The defence liaison agrees, then points to a reservation his staff still consider insufficient. The committee can publish the unresolved objection or retain it inside a signed professional return. Neither choice changes what the work actually cost. The question is who will be allowed to recognize the same mistake when the next undertaking begins to travel between departments."""),

Branch(TAG,'contact_verification','air_watch','2006.1.1',('radar','communications'),(
 ('Keep the Uncertain Track',"""The liaison exercise ends with an unidentified contact still marked unidentified. A senior observer calls the result inconclusive. The controller replies that the contact was inconclusive before the exercise began, and nobody acquired the evidence needed to change that. Begin a national review of how uncertainty travels from observation to instruction. Civil aviation, air defence and the receiving offices need a shared way to ask a second question without silently cancelling the first warning. The aim is a dependable account of what was known, not a reassuring diagram in which every track acquires a convenient answer."""),
 ('Require a Second Account',"""A second account is not an instruction to wait forever. It names which observation is missing and who can obtain it, allowing a controller to preserve a warning while challenging an unjustified identification. Give that practice a civil verification mandate. The additional hearings and explanations consume political attention, particularly when a confident response would have been easier to announce. In return, professional work can proceed on a record that distinguishes a confirmed contact from a feared one. The unanswered question remains visible to the person who will eventually authorize a response."""),
 ('Protect the Response Chain',"""The protected response chain gives an authorized duty officer a clear route through competing reports. It does not make that officer's first interpretation infallible. Require a signed countercheck inside the chain and retain the observation that caused it, even when the eventual instruction must remain restricted. This policy reserves civilian capacity for professional readiness. Its advantage is a dependable receiving responsibility; its cost is work that other offices cannot claim during the protected period. A fast instruction will be judged by the evidence it carried, rather than the number of desks it managed to bypass."""),
 ('Exercise the Countercheck',"""The next review begins with two deliberately incompatible reports and a receiving officer who is not allowed to reconcile them by deleting either one. Request a fresh funded period through the existing air-watch service. The selected civil or protected lane must actually deliver that work after this instruction. Old exercise results remain useful background, but cannot certify the present chain. At the end, the office must show where the second account entered, who answered it, and whether an urgent instruction reached its recipient with its uncertainty still attached."""),
 ('Hear the Controller Again',"""The controller is invited back after the funded period, before its results have been condensed into a favourable summary. She identifies one warning that was improved by a second account and another that remained unresolved. The committee asks whether the receiving chain understood the difference. Its review uses completed work, including the delays that the selected policy accepted. An interrupted exercise provides no substitute for that return. The useful finding may be a limit rather than a success: there are instructions the government should be unable to issue confidently until somebody answers the person listening at the first desk."""),
 ('Publish the Unanswered Question',"""The public version of the review will contain an unanswered question. A press officer worries that it will become the only sentence anybody remembers. The controller answers that a confident sentence would be less accurate, and considerably easier to misuse later. Publish the record with its limitation. The choice supports civil confidence through an account that can be challenged, while taking continued political work to explain. It does not disclose every professional procedure. It establishes that uncertainty itself is not misconduct, and that an honest warning need not be converted into a certainty before it is allowed to matter."""),
 ('Keep the Signed Countercheck',"""The duty staff propose a signed internal countercheck, available to the next officer who must use the response chain. They accept that restricted circulation creates a responsibility to preserve the dissenting report rather than merely mentioning that somebody disagreed. Adopt that professional return with its continuing civilian opportunity cost. The resulting record can support a later verified channel, but only because the countercheck was actually exercised and assessed. Neither secrecy nor an urgent tone can stand in for the missing work. The government is keeping a professional answer, not purchasing permission to stop asking questions."""),
 ('An Identification Worth Keeping',"""The standing identification procedure now tells the receiving officer what remains unknown. It preserves the selected public account or signed countercheck, together with the cost that made it possible to sustain. A completed national review can support future dialogue even if another government refuses to answer. It does not establish that an incident has occurred, or that a particular aircraft belongs to any country. Its achievement is smaller and more durable: when a warning becomes an instruction, the person expected to obey can find the evidence, the limitation and the name of somebody responsible for both."""),
),(
 ('Independent contact verification',{'air_mission_efficiency':.02},{'political_power_gain':-.035}),
 ('Protected response countercheck',{'air_mission_efficiency':.025},{'consumer_goods_factor':.004}),
),
"""The observer draws a neat line from the report to the proposed instruction. The controller draws another line back to a question nobody answered. 'If you remove that one, the diagram is easier,' she says. 'The work is not.' A liaison officer admits that the recipient would otherwise assume the second account had been received. The room grows quieter. They can expose the unresolved question to wider scrutiny or keep a signed professional countercheck within the response chain. Either route must give the next duty officer something more useful than confidence borrowed from a clean sheet of paper.""",
"""The exercise report includes the controller's original hesitation. It also includes the answer that arrived in time to change one receiving instruction, and the question that remained open when the period ended. The duty officer reads all three. 'I would rather inherit this than a perfect result,' he says. A representative asks who else should be allowed to see why. The controller leaves the choice to the hearing, but insists that neither account should turn the unresolved contact into a confirmed one. The completed work has earned a procedure; it has not earned a convenient history."""),

Branch(TAG,'replacement_standards','procurement_scrutiny','2006.1.1',('tools','industry'),(
 ('The Part beside the Invoice',"""A returned component is placed beside an invoice marked accepted. The workshop can explain how the acceptance happened; the receiving service can explain why it should not have happened that way. Both explanations are necessary. Open a review of replacement standards that follows an order into actual use, rather than ending where a purchasing office obtained the required signature. The existing industrial and procurement settlements provide the starting responsibility. The next question is who may challenge an acceptance after the paperwork has become somebody else's evidence that a commitment was fulfilled."""),
 ('Protect Independent Acceptance',"""The independent examiner wants enough time to refuse a delivery without first negotiating with the office that promised it. Give that examination a protected civil claim. It improves the continuity of receiving work, while demanding political effort whenever an objection delays a publicly announced schedule. The examiner must describe a specific defect and a practical route to a new decision. Independence does not mean an unlimited hold on everybody else's work. It means the person responsible for accepting a replacement is allowed to say that a deadline did not repair the part placed on the bench."""),
 ('Guarantee Replacement Windows',"""The receiving services ask for a predictable window in which replacements, technical staff and acceptance authority are available together. Protect that schedule, recording the civilian work it displaces. A workshop should not finish an order only to discover that its examiner has been reassigned to another urgent programme. The policy supports dependable production through coordination, rather than a claim that an instruction can create equipment. A returned part still has to be repaired or replaced. The guarantee concerns who will receive it and who must answer if the next window is also allowed to disappear."""),
 ('Trace One Complete Order',"""Select an ordinary replacement order and follow its receiving record through a fresh funded procurement-scrutiny period. The committee is not asking the workshop to stage a perfect demonstration. It wants to see a challenge handled while the rest of the schedule continues to exist. The selected lane must complete its work after the present request. Earlier acceptance reports remain in the file, but cannot certify the current procedure. Trace who discovered the defect, who could refuse the delivery, and which other claimant was told that an honest refusal would alter the timetable."""),
 ('Read the Returned Order',"""The completed review contains a returned order with its original acceptance still visible. A procurement officer asks whether the old signature should be crossed out. The examiner says it should be explained, because otherwise the same mistake will look like a different mistake next time. Compare the selected policy with the genuinely completed receiving period. The account must include the replacement that passed, the one that did not, and the cost of keeping the necessary people available together. A cancelled period cannot provide that evidence, however persuasive its interim description may have sounded."""),
 ('Release the Acceptance Finding',"""Publish the acceptance finding without publishing every protected technical detail. The receiving service can show why a signature was challenged, what the examiner required, and which schedule changed as a result. That account supports public confidence while taking political attention to maintain. It will be inconvenient when a later order fails for a similar reason. The examiner accepts that inconvenience as part of the arrangement. A public standard is useful because a workshop, claimant or successor can recognize when the government has begun treating it as an optional explanation for somebody else's delay."""),
 ('Keep the Workshop Account',"""The workshop proposes a professional account detailed enough for the next examiner to repeat the acceptance check. Its supervisors want the failed attempt retained beside the corrected one, despite a purchasing office's preference for a shorter report. Preserve that return within the protected receiving schedule. It improves professional continuity while continuing to reserve civilian capacity for the work. The account cannot claim that every claimant approved the resulting priority. Its value is that an apprentice can see which decision changed, which evidence changed it, and why a signed invoice did not settle the question."""),
 ('A Replacement Standard That Lasts',"""The standing replacement standard follows an order beyond the purchasing signature. It retains the selected independent or protected receiving practice and the burden accepted to sustain it. Completed scrutiny improves that practice without promising an equipment delivery that production has not supplied. If an overseas supplier refuses an exchange or disappears from the schedule, the domestic acceptance method still works. The committee leaves room for that possibility in its final account. A dependable standard is one the receiving office can use when the convenient partner, the helpful supervisor or the first promised window is no longer available."""),
),(
 ('Independent replacement acceptance',{'production_factory_efficiency_gain_factor':.025},{'political_power_gain':-.03}),
 ('Protected replacement schedule',{'production_factory_max_efficiency_factor':.015},{'consumer_goods_factor':.005}),
),
"""The examiner sets the returned component on the table before anyone can begin with the totals. 'This was accepted,' she says. The procurement officer nods. 'It was also returned.' A workshop supervisor asks that neither sentence be omitted. They can publish the finding or retain a detailed professional return, but both versions need to explain the point at which a signature stopped being an adequate answer. The supervisor has brought an apprentice to listen. He will inherit the method long after the people defending this particular order have moved to other desks.""",
"""The apprentice can repeat the acceptance check without being told which answer will please the committee. That is the examiner's strongest finding. The procurement officer has a different one: a failed delivery was returned without the receiving schedule collapsing into unofficial promises. Both want the result preserved, although they disagree about its audience. The committee must decide whether to publish an explanation or retain the detailed workshop account. Either choice carries a continuing burden. Neither permits a corrected order to erase the people whose waiting time made the correction possible."""),

Branch(TAG,'pacific_channel','civil_space','2006.1.1',('satellite','diplomacy'),(
 ('A Channel for a Question',"""A scientific question arrives in a diplomatic envelope and spends a week being treated as an invitation. By the time the researchers see it, the sending office has assumed that silence means reluctance. Establish a receiving channel that distinguishes a technical question, an offer and a commitment. The existing civil-space settlement provides a domestic method even when no partner is available. Where an earned debris-cooperation record exists, it can provide useful shared experience. Neither record obliges another government to agree, or allows a receiving clerk to answer a political question with a scientific signature."""),
 ('Share the Method and Its Limit',"""The researchers propose sharing the method together with a statement of what it cannot establish. A diplomatic officer worries that the limitation will sound less impressive than the promised exchange. The receiving secretary replies that an impressive misunderstanding would be harder to correct later. Adopt an open scientific channel with the political burden of explaining its boundaries. Domestic researchers retain the same method if cooperation is declined. The policy does not require the government to reveal protected information. It requires the government to stop describing an unanswered invitation as evidence of a scientific agreement."""),
 ('Keep a Bounded Technical Channel',"""A restricted technical channel may be useful when the subject cannot be discussed in an open exchange. Give it a bounded receiving authority and keep the unanswered questions beside the accepted ones. The protected schedule improves professional work while reserving civilian capacity that other services will have to surrender. It cannot turn an old partnership record into permanent consent. Each new contact must still concern eligible participants and a question the receiving office is authorized to answer. If contact ends, the domestic examination continues with an honest note about the information it did not receive."""),
 ('Test the Receiving Procedure',"""Request a fresh civil-space period to test how an inquiry reaches the person able to answer it. Begin with domestic material, so a foreign refusal cannot make the review impossible. The selected civil or protected lane must deliver its actual funded work after this request. Where an established joint record is relevant, mark the part it contributed without claiming that a completed debris programme is a new operational capability. The final receiving return should show who could answer, which authority they used, and what happened when a question remained outside that authority."""),
 ('Separate Answer from Assent',"""The completed period has produced an answer, an unanswered question and an invitation that nobody accepted. The receiving office lists them separately. A diplomat admits that the earlier draft described all three as progress toward agreement. Review the actual work before that wording becomes a standing assumption. The chosen channel should make scientific communication more dependable without asking technical staff to manufacture political consent. Its usefulness survives an interrupted partnership: a domestic office can still identify what it knows, what it requested, and which part of the requested account another country declined to provide."""),
 ('Publish the Limited Finding',"""Make the limited finding available to the people who will have to explain it. The public account records which methods were examined, where cooperation contributed, and which invitation remained unanswered. Maintaining that distinction takes political effort, particularly when a broad announcement would attract more attention. It also gives a future receiving office permission to say that a useful exchange stopped short of agreement. The researchers are willing to sign that account. They would rather have their actual answer remembered than be thanked for a promise the government made while their question was still travelling between departments."""),
 ('Retain the Signed Exchange',"""The restricted return will retain the actual inquiry and answer together, signed by the responsible receiving offices. It will also retain the unanswered request. A technical liaison argues that this detail is exactly what makes restricted cooperation repeatable: the next officer can see which silence was a refusal and which was simply a question sent to the wrong desk. Adopt that professional record with its continuing civilian burden. It provides an earned channel for later verification, but cannot oblige a partner to remain eligible or authorize a new exchange on somebody else's old signature."""),
 ('A Channel That Can Say No',"""The standing channel has a useful final entry: the person authorized to decline a question. Researchers no longer have to conceal a refusal inside a technical answer, and diplomats no longer have to describe every received letter as an agreement. Retain the selected open or protected practice, improving it through the completed review while keeping its cost. Cooperation may support a later effort to verify an incident, but the method remains useful without it. The government has earned a way to ask a precise question and receive an honest answer, including an answer it did not want."""),
),(
 ('Open scientific receiving method',{'research_speed_factor':.015},{'political_power_gain':-.03}),
 ('Bounded technical exchange',{'air_mission_efficiency':.015},{'consumer_goods_factor':.004}),
),
"""The researcher asks which part of the envelope made a question into an invitation. The diplomatic clerk looks at the covering note and admits that it was probably his summary. 'Then let us keep the question,' she says, 'and correct the invitation.' A liaison suggests a restricted receiving chain; another asks for a public account of the method and its limitations. There is no foreign representative in the room, and nobody is authorized to speak for one. Their first choice concerns how Osea will receive an answer, or record that no answer has arrived.""",
"""The return contains three separate headings. Beneath the first is work completed by domestic staff. Beneath the second is material received through an established cooperative record. Beneath the third is an unanswered inquiry. The diplomat reads them without combining the totals. 'It sounds smaller,' he says. The researcher smiles. 'It is the size we can defend.' The committee can publish that limited account or retain a signed technical exchange. The decision changes who can examine the record, but cannot change another country's answer or restore a partnership that the receiving offices no longer have."""),

Branch(TAG,'emergency_review','crisis_authority','2010.1.1',('security','ballot'),(
 ('The Authority after Midnight',"""An emergency instruction is copied into the next day's ordinary schedule because nobody has written down who may end it. The duty officer says the risk has not disappeared. The civil clerk says that was not the question. Review the authority that survives after an urgent decision, building on the government's existing crisis settlement. The work is useful during peace, external danger or a prolonged conflict. It concerns which offices may renew an exception, what evidence they need, and how a receiving service learns that a temporary priority has begun consuming a permanent part of its timetable."""),
 ('Require Civil Renewal',"""The civil renewal procedure gives an exception a return date and a receiving office able to challenge its continuation. Its officials must explain a refusal without implying that the original emergency was imaginary. That is demanding political work, and it can reduce the military margin available for an immediate response. Accept the burden in exchange for a more dependable civil account. A renewal hearing does not decide every operational question. It decides whether the government can still identify the authority it is using, the purpose it serves, and the work it continues to postpone."""),
 ('Keep a Limited Emergency Mandate',"""Protect a limited emergency mandate with a named professional authority and a signed account of its continuing use. The duty staff gain a dependable chain for urgent work, while the government accepts the political cost of defending that priority. The mandate cannot expand merely because another office finds it convenient. It must be reviewed against current conditions, including a return to peace or the failure of a foreign dialogue. Retaining the instruction is a choice that somebody must renew, rather than a conclusion silently produced by leaving yesterday's exceptional order inside today's ordinary schedule."""),
 ('Review the Present Exception',"""Open a fresh review through the corresponding crisis-authority service. The government is asking about the exception operating now, so a previous completed period cannot certify it. The chosen lane must finish its funded work after the request. During war that work may be reduced domestic continuity; its return must say so. The receiving secretary will retain the current authority, the reason for renewal and the services that bear its burden. If the review is interrupted, the next request begins from the present date instead of treating a cancelled account as an answer."""),
 ('Record What Still Requires It',"""The completed return identifies an exception that remains necessary and another that survived only because its recipients did not know who could revoke it. The committee refuses to describe either finding as a verdict on the entire government. Review the actual receiving work and retain the selected civil or protected explanation. Peace may change the reason for an authority without answering every domestic obligation it created. A prolonged conflict may justify continued priority without making its cost disappear. The useful record names the remaining purpose closely enough that a successor will be able to recognize when it ends."""),
 ('A Mandate That Can Be Ended',"""The standing procedure names an ending authority as clearly as an issuing authority. Officials can retain the chosen civil or protected mandate, improve its practice through the completed review, and continue paying the burden that sustains it. They can also explain why a particular exception no longer applies. The settlement does not require a foreign acceptance, a war outcome or a historical deadline to be useful. It leaves the next duty officer a narrower instruction and the receiving services a way to ask whether the government still intends to use it tomorrow."""),
),(
 ('Civil renewal of exceptions',{'stability_factor':.02},{'army_org_factor':-.005}),
 ('Limited emergency mandate',{'army_org_factor':.02},{'political_power_gain':-.04}),
),
"""The duty officer has worked through the night and hears the proposed hearing as a criticism of the decision that kept his service functioning. The civil clerk waits before answering. 'I am asking who can stop renewing it,' she says. He studies the copied instruction and finds no name. A protected mandate could provide one inside the professional chain; civil renewal could expose the same decision to a wider challenge. Neither would make last night's work disappear. The committee must choose how a necessary exception will be prevented from becoming an instruction nobody remembers choosing.""",
"""The return is read in the same room, during an ordinary working day. The duty officer points to one instruction that still needs protection and another that his own staff have already replaced. The clerk records both. Their agreement is more limited than the headline either side could have requested. It concerns who will answer the next renewal, and what evidence that person must receive. The committee may keep a public or professional account, but the completed period has made one thing difficult to conceal: continuing an exception is an active national choice with people still waiting at its edge."""),

Branch(TAG,'federal_account','appropriations','2011.1.1',('document','peace'),(
 ('Bring Back the First Account',"""The first appropriation account is returned with newer receiving reports attached by different clerks. Some of its conditions survived; others were amended, challenged or interrupted. Begin a concluding review only after the guarantor and identification branches have produced actual returns of their own. The government is not being asked to announce that every difficulty has ended. It is being asked to explain which promises it still intends to keep. That explanation remains necessary in peace, during an unresolved conflict, or after another country has ceased to be an eligible partner for the hoped-for exchange."""),
 ('Protect the Civil Remainder',"""The civil remainder is the work that continued after urgent commitments took their first share. Its officers ask the government to protect it openly rather than praise its ingenuity and reserve the same people again. Give that continuity a standing civil priority with a continuing political cost. The choice does not erase the protected undertakings accepted earlier. It identifies who will receive the next available period and who must explain a further postponement. A national account is more credible when its ordinary obligations remain visible after the exceptional ones have become familiar."""),
 ('Retain a Bounded Readiness Claim',"""The readiness representatives accept that a final account cannot describe every protected undertaking as equally urgent. They propose retaining a bounded claim that can actually be received and assessed. Civil services will continue to bear the resulting opportunity cost. Choose that priority without turning it into another unlimited promise. The earlier public or professional records remain attached, including their disagreements. The government can sustain readiness through an honest description of the work it has reserved; it does not need to claim that a foreign dialogue succeeded, that a conflict ended, or that every displaced claimant accepted the outcome."""),
 ('Request the Successor Period',"""Request a fresh appropriation period to test the responsibility the next administration would inherit. The selected existing service must perform the work after this request, using its ordinary national reservations and actual costs. The committee wants a receiving account that can be repeated without its current members sitting beside every desk. Old completed work explains how the procedure developed; it does not supply the successor's return. If the period is interrupted, acknowledge the interruption and reopen a current request rather than filling the missing pages with the confidence of the earlier settlement."""),
 ('Leave the Unfinished Page',"""The successor period has produced a completed return and an unfinished page listing obligations outside its scope. A secretary offers to place the page in another file. The chair asks that it remain attached. Review the funded work without claiming it answered every national question. The account can recognize useful civil continuity or protected readiness while preserving the burden that its chosen priority imposed. This is a conclusion about the government's method of keeping promises, not a declaration that the surrounding world has accepted them. Its unfinished page is part of the explanation a successor deserves to receive."""),
 ('An Account for the Next Shift',"""Keep the selected national priority as a standing account, improved by the work that actually reached its receiving offices. Its continuing political or civilian cost remains alongside the benefit. The next shift will inherit the signed decisions, the unanswered inquiries and the record of work deliberately postponed. It may begin after the expected calendar, under different external conditions, or with partners who no longer exist in the same relationship. None of that makes the account useless. Its value is that a successor can change a promise without first having to discover which part of it the government quietly stopped keeping."""),
),(
 ('Civil continuity remainder',{'repair_speed_factor':.02},{'political_power_gain':-.035}),
 ('Bounded readiness remainder',{'production_factory_efficiency_gain_factor':.02},{'consumer_goods_factor':.004}),
),
"""The oldest folder is placed at the end of the table, not the beginning. A younger clerk asks why a concluded account has returned. 'Because people are still receiving it,' the transport officer replies. The new reports do not all agree, but they identify the same limits more clearly than the original assurance. The committee can protect the civil remainder or retain a bounded readiness claim. Neither route permits it to tear out the earlier disagreements. The clerk opens a clean page for the next period and leaves the old signatures where their successors will be able to find them.""",
"""The final receiving report is short enough for the next shift to read before work begins. The clerk recognizes the cancelled window from the first hearing and finds the explanation beside it. 'Somebody will still disagree with this,' she says. The transport officer nods. That is why the names and conditions remain. The government can publish the account or retain a signed professional return, with the continuing burden of its choice. It cannot promise that every partner will answer or every danger will pass. It can leave an honest description of the responsibilities it has actually chosen to keep."""),
)
